Terraform Basics

Infrastructure as Code tool by HashiCorp — provision cloud infra using config files.


What is Terraform?

Defines infrastructure using HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language)
Cloud-agnostic — AWS, Azure, GCP, and more
Declarative — you say what you want, Terraform figures out how
Tracks infrastructure using a state file


Core Workflow
init → validate → plan → apply → destroy
CommandWhat it doesterraform initDownload providers & set up projectterraform validateCheck syntax errorsterraform fmtFormat codeterraform planPreview what will changeterraform applyCreate / update infrastructureterraform destroyDelete all managed infrastructure

Key Concepts
TermMeaningProviderPlugin to talk to a cloud (AWS, Azure, etc.)ResourceA piece of infra (EC2, S3, VPC...)StateFile that tracks what Terraform createdModuleReusable group of resourcesData SourceRead existing infra (not managed by TF)OutputExpose values after applyVariableInput to make config flexibleBackendWhere state file is stored

Provider
hclterraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

Resource
hclresource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "MyServer"
  }
}

Variables
hcl# Declare
variable "instance_type" {
  type    = string
  default = "t2.micro"
}

# Use
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  instance_type = var.instance_type
}
Set values in terraform.tfvars:
hclinstance_type = "t3.micro"

Outputs
hcloutput "public_ip" {
  value = aws_instance.web.public_ip
}
bashterraform output public_ip

State

Stored in terraform.tfstate
Tracks all resources Terraform manages
Use remote backend (S3, Terraform Cloud) for team work

bashterraform state list          # List resources
terraform state show <name>   # Show resource details

Modules
Reusable blocks of config.
hclmodule "vpc" {
  source  = "terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws"
  version = "5.0.0"

  name = "my-vpc"
  cidr = "10.0.0.0/16"
}

count vs for_each
hcl# count — numbered
resource "aws_instance" "server" {
  count         = 3
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  tags          = { Name = "server-${count.index}" }
}

# for_each — named (preferred)
resource "aws_instance" "server" {
  for_each      = toset(["web", "api", "db"])
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  tags          = { Name = each.key }
}

Conditional & locals
hcllocals {
  env           = "prod"
  instance_type = local.env == "prod" ? "t3.large" : "t2.micro"
}

Remote Backend (S3)
hclterraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "my-tf-state"
    key            = "prod/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "tf-state-lock"
    encrypt        = true
  }
}

Interview Quick Hits
What is Terraform state?
A JSON file that maps config to real resources. Needed to track what exists and detect changes.
Terraform vs Ansible?
Terraform = provisioning infra. Ansible = configuring software on servers.
What is state locking?
Prevents two people from running apply at the same time. Uses DynamoDB with S3 backend.
count vs for_each?
for_each is preferred — removing an item in count causes all later resources to be recreated.
What is a data source?
Reads existing infra not managed by Terraform. Uses data {} block.
What is drift?
When real infra differs from Terraform state (e.g., manual changes). Detected by terraform plan.
